Index	SS / Module	Test Case Name	Feature	Meta Headers	Meta Macros	Meta Arrays	Speed	Mode	Memory Start Offset	Memory End Offset	Meta Test Description	Test Description	Meta Test Steps / Procedure	Test Steps / Procedure	Meta Impacted Registers	Impacted Registers	Meta Validation / Acceptance Criteria	Validation / Acceptance Criteria	Remarks
1	GPIO	gpio_reg_wr_rd_test	Register Write-Read Verification	<stdio.h>; <stdlib.h>; "test_common.h"; "test_define.c"; <gpio/gpio_def.h>; <gpio/gpio_offset.h>	SOFT_RST_REG_DATA; CNT	addr_array[49]; default_value_array[49]; read_mask_array[49]; write_mask_array[49]; skip_array[49]; skip_rst_array[49]; chk_val[6]	NA	NA	NA	NA	This testcase performs two phases of register validation on GPIO GP0 registers (MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10). Phase 1 (chk_rst_val): Iterates over addr_array[49], skips entries where skip_rst_array[i]==1 or read_mask_array[i]==0x00000000, reads each register via read_reg(addr), masks the read data with 0xfffffffe, and compares against default_value_array[i]. Mismatches increment def_fail_cnt. Phase 2 (chk_rd_wr): Iterates over 6 test patterns in chk_val[6]={0xffffffff, 0xaaaaaaaa, 0x55555555, 0xf5f5f5f5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xffff0000}. For each pattern, writes (data_wr & write_mask_array[i]) to each register via write_reg(addr, ...), skipping entries where skip_array[i]==1 or write_mask_array[i]==0x00000000. Then reads back each register via read_reg(addr), masks with read_mask_array[i], computes expected value as ((data_wr & read_mask_array[i] & write_mask_array[i]) | (wr_n & read_mask_array[i] & default_value_array[i])) where wr_n = (write_mask_array[i] ^ 0xffffffff), and compares. Mismatches increment wr_fail_cnt. Finally, test_case() calls finish(1) if either fail counter is > 0, otherwise finish(0).	This test validates the GPIO GP0 registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) in two phases. Phase 1 verifies that each register's default (reset) value matches the expected default value after applying a read mask. Phase 2 writes six distinct test patterns to each register (respecting write masks), reads back the values (respecting read masks), computes the expected result considering writable and non-writable bit fields, and verifies correctness. The test passes only if all default value checks and all write-read checks succeed across all registers and all patterns.	1. Enter test_case(). 2. Call chk_rst_val(): For i=0 to CNT-1, set addr=addr_array[i]. If skip_rst_array[i]==1, skip (continue). If read_mask_array[i]==0x00000000, skip (continue). Read data_rd=read_reg(addr). Compute data=(data_rd & 0xfffffffe). Compare data against default_value_array[i]. If mismatch, increment def_fail_cnt and print failure. 3. Call chk_rd_wr(): Initialize chk_val[6]={0xffffffff, 0xaaaaaaaa, 0x55555555, 0xf5f5f5f5, 0xA5A5A5A5, 0xffff0000}. For j=0 to 5, set data_wr=chk_val[j]. 3a. Write phase: For i=0 to CNT-1, set addr=addr_array[i]. If skip_array[i]==1, skip. If write_mask_array[i]==0x00000000, skip. Else write_reg(addr, (data_wr & write_mask_array[i])). 3b. Read-verify phase: For i=0 to CNT-1, set addr=addr_array[i]. If skip_array[i]==1, skip. If write_mask_array[i]==0x00000000, skip. If read_mask_array[i]==0x00000000, skip. Else read data_rd=(read_reg(addr) & read_mask_array[i]). Compute wr_n=(write_mask_array[i] ^ 0xffffffff). Compute exp_val=((data_wr & read_mask_array[i] & write_mask_array[i]) | (wr_n & read_mask_array[i] & default_value_array[i])). Compare data_rd against exp_val. If mismatch, increment wr_fail_cnt. 4. Check results: If def_fail_cnt > 0 or wr_fail_cnt > 0, call finish(1) (fail). Else call finish(0) (pass).	1. Read the default (reset) value of each GPIO GP0 register (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10), skipping registers flagged in the reset-skip list or those that are not readable.\n2. Mask each read value and compare against the expected default value for that register. Record any mismatches.\n3. For each of six predefined test patterns, write the pattern to each register after applying the write mask, skipping registers flagged in the skip list or those that are not writable.\n4. After writing each pattern, read back each register value, applying the read mask.\n5. Compute the expected read-back value by combining the written data (masked by both read and write masks) with the default value for non-writable bits.\n6. Compare the actual read-back value against the computed expected value. Record any mismatches.\n7. Repeat steps 3 through 6 for all six test patterns.\n8. Determine overall test result: pass if no default-value mismatches and no write-read mismatches occurred; fail otherwise.	MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10	gp0_gpio_8; gp0_gpio_9; gp0_gpio_10	Phase 1 (Default Value Check): For each register in addr_array (MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_9, MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_10), read_reg(addr) is called, result is masked with 0xfffffffe, and compared to default_value_array[i]. If mismatch, def_fail_cnt is incremented. Phase 2 (Write-Read Check): For each of 6 patterns in chk_val[6], write_reg(addr, data_wr & write_mask_array[i]) is called, then read_reg(addr) is called and masked with read_mask_array[i]. Expected value is computed as ((data_wr & read_mask_array[i] & write_mask_array[i]) | (wr_n & read_mask_array[i] & default_value_array[i])). If data_rd != exp_val, wr_fail_cnt is incremented. Final pass condition: def_fail_cnt == 0 AND wr_fail_cnt == 0 results in finish(0) (pass). Any non-zero fail counter results in finish(1) (fail).	1. All GPIO GP0 registers (gp0_gpio_8, gp0_gpio_9, gp0_gpio_10) must return their expected default values when read after reset, after applying the read mask.\n2. For each of the six test patterns written to each register, the read-back value (masked by the read mask) must match the expected value computed from the written data, write mask, read mask, and default values for non-writable bits.\n3. The test passes (finish with success) only if zero default-value mismatches and zero write-read mismatches are recorded across all registers and all test patterns.\n4. Any single mismatch in either phase causes the test to fail (finish with failure).	The test uses a skip mechanism via skip_array and skip_rst_array to selectively bypass certain register indices during write-read and default-value checks respectively. The read data in the default value check phase is masked with a fixed mask that clears bit 0 before comparison. Six distinct bit patterns are used to exercise various bit combinations across writable fields. The soft_reset_chk function is disabled (inside #ifdef 0 block) and is not executed. A comment in test_define.c notes that the din value may automatically become 1 if not forced, affecting level select behavior and default value matching.
2	GPIO	test_gpio_level_sel_intr_en	GPIO Level-Select Interrupt Enable Verification	<lss_sysreg.h>; <stdio.h>; <test_define.c>; <test_common.h>; <gpio/gpio_def.h>; <gpio/gpio_offset.h>	CNT; GPIO0; GPIO1; DEBUG_DISPLAY	addr_array[49]; default_value_array[49]; read_mask_array[49]; write_mask_array[49]; skip_array[49]	NA	NA	NA	NA	This testcase validates GPIO level-select interrupt functionality for GPIOs 8-39. It configures each GPIO in input mode with level interrupt enabled via MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), enables group interrupts via MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1, enables sysreg interrupt via MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1, and triggers an SRAM write to 0xA0243ffc. Phase 1 (Active High Level): For i=0 to 31, writes 0x00180000 to MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4) setting io_ctrl=1 (input mode) and level_sel=1 (active high level interrupt). Enables group interrupt with wr_val=(1<<i) to MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1. Writes 0xffffffff to 0xA0243ffc. Waits for interrupt (int_pend==1). In Default_IRQHandler: reads MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), checks bit 1 (intr_raw_sts) is set, reads MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1 to verify group interrupt bit (1<<i) is set, clears interrupt by writing 0x00110000 to MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), reads back and verifies value is 0x100001, disables group interrupt by writing 0x00000000 to MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1, reads MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1 to verify cleared to 0x0, clears sysreg status via MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1 and verifies. Phase 2 (Active Low Level): For i=0 to 31, writes 0x00100000 to MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4) setting io_ctrl=1 (input mode) and level_sel=0 (active low level interrupt). Same interrupt enable, trigger, and verification flow as Phase 1 but with ~(wr_val) written to 0xA0243ffc. Final result: finish(test_err) where test_err is incremented on any failure.	This test validates the GPIO level-select interrupt enable functionality for GPIO pins 8 through 39. It verifies that level-triggered interrupts are correctly generated, detected, and cleared for both active-high and active-low level selections. Each GPIO is configured in input mode, the appropriate level-select bit is programmed, group interrupts are enabled, and the test waits for the interrupt service routine to confirm correct interrupt behavior including raw status assertion, group status assertion, interrupt clearing, and sysreg status clearing.	1. Enter test_case(). 2. Enable GIC IRQ (87 for GPIO0, 88 for GPIO1). 3. Enable sysreg interrupt: write_reg(MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1, LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1_GPIO0_INTR) for GPIO0 or LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1_GPIO1_INTR for GPIO1. 4. Phase 1 (Active High Level): For i=0 to 31: 4a. Compute wr_val=(1<<i). 4b. write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), 0x00180000) sets io_ctrl=1 (input), level_sel=1 (active high). 4c. wait_on(50). 4d. write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1, wr_val) enable group interrupt for GPIO i. 4e. wait_on(10). 4f. write_reg(0xA0243ffc, 0xffffffff) trigger via SRAM. 4g. Set int_pend=1, wait in while loop until int_pend==0 (cleared by ISR). 5. Phase 2 (Active Low Level): For i=0 to 31: 5a-5g same as Phase 1 but write 0x00100000 (level_sel=0) and write ~(wr_val) to 0xA0243ffc. 6. ISR (Default_IRQHandler): 6a. write_reg(0xA0243ffc, 0xffffffff). 6b. rdata=read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4)). 6c. Check (rdata & 0x2) != 0x0. 6d. rdata_grp=read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1). Check (rdata_grp & (1<<i)) != 0. 6e. write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), 0x00110000). 6f. wait_on(20). 6g. rdata=read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4)). Check rdata==0x100001. 6h. write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1, 0x00000000). 6i. rdata_grp=read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1). Check rdata_grp==0x0. 6j. write_reg(MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1, ...). 6k. rdata=read_reg(MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1). Verify cleared. 6l. GIC_ClearIRQ. 7. finish(test_err).	1. Enable the GIC interrupt line for the GPIO block and enable the sysreg interrupt for GPIO.\n2. For each GPIO pin (8 through 39), configure the pin in input mode with active-high level interrupt enabled by writing the appropriate configuration value to the GPIO register.\n3. Enable the group interrupt for the specific GPIO pin.\n4. Trigger the interrupt condition by writing to the SRAM location and wait for the interrupt service routine to execute.\n5. In the ISR, verify that the interrupt raw status bit is set in the GPIO register for the active pin.\n6. Verify that the group interrupt status bit corresponding to the active pin is set in the group interrupt status register.\n7. Clear the interrupt by writing to the interrupt clear bit in the GPIO register and verify the register reads back the expected cleared value.\n8. Disable the group interrupt and verify the group interrupt status register is cleared to zero.\n9. Clear the sysreg interrupt status and verify it is cleared.\n10. Repeat steps 2 through 9 for all 32 GPIO pins with active-high level selection.\n11. Repeat the entire sequence for all 32 GPIO pins with active-low level selection (level_sel=0).\n12. Determine overall test result: pass if no errors were recorded across all pins and both level-select phases; fail otherwise.	MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1; 0xA0243ffc; MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1; MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1	gp0_gpio_8; gp0_intr1_intr_en1; gp0_intr1_intr_sts1; MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1 (external); MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1 (external); 0xA0243ffc (SRAM)	For each GPIO pin i (0 to 31) in both active-high and active-low phases: 1. After interrupt trigger, read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4)) must have bit 1 (intr_raw_sts) set: (rdata & 0x2) != 0x0. 2. read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1) must have bit i set: (rdata_grp & (1<<i)) != 0. 3. After clearing interrupt via write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8+(i*4), 0x00110000), read_reg must return 0x100001. 4. After disabling group interrupt via write_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_EN1, 0x00000000), read_reg(MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_INTR1_INTR_STS1) must return 0x0. 5. After clearing sysreg status via write_reg(MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1, ...), read_reg(MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1) must have the GPIO interrupt bit cleared. 6. Final pass condition: test_err == 0 results in finish(0) (pass). Any non-zero test_err results in finish(test_err) (fail).	1. For each GPIO pin, the interrupt raw status bit must be asserted after the level-triggered interrupt condition is met.\n2. The group interrupt status register must reflect the correct bit set for the active GPIO pin.\n3. After writing the interrupt clear bit, the GPIO register must read back the expected cleared value (0x100001).\n4. After disabling the group interrupt, the group interrupt status register must read as zero.\n5. The sysreg interrupt status must be successfully cleared after each interrupt servicing.\n6. All checks must pass for all 32 GPIO pins in both active-high and active-low level-select configurations.\n7. The test passes only if zero errors are accumulated across all pins and both phases.	The test exercises both active-high (level_sel=1, value 0x00180000) and active-low (level_sel=0, value 0x00100000) level interrupt configurations. The SRAM address 0xA0243ffc is used as a trigger mechanism to simulate the interrupt condition. The test uses GIC IRQ 87 for GPIO0 and IRQ 88 for GPIO1 via conditional compilation (#ifdef GPIO0/#ifdef GPIO1). The MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_INTR_EN1 and MIZAR_LSS_SYSREG_RAW_STCR1 registers are external to the GPIO IP (belong to LSS SYSREG) and are used for system-level interrupt routing and status clearing. The code accesses GPIO registers 8 through 39 using base address MIZAR_GPIO_GP0_GPIO_8 with computed offsets (i*4) in a loop.